1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://cbdb.fas.harvard.edu/cbdbapi/person.php?id=31005 '''Tuoba Gui''' 拓跋珪] [31005] Giles, p. 740. — [https://www.geni.com/projects/CBDB-Hartwell-collection/46406 RMH]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_Daowu_of_Northern_Wei
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Daowu of Northern Wei ((北)魏道武帝; 4 August 371[2] – 6 November 409[3]), personal name Tuoba Gui (拓拔珪), né Tuoba Shegui (拓拔渉珪), was the founding emperor of the Northern Wei dynasty of China.[4] He was the grandson of the last
2 CONC  prince of Dai, Tuoba Shiyiqian. After the fall of the Dai state to the Former Qin in 376, he was presumed to be the eventual successor to the Dai throne. After the Former Qin fell into disarray in 383 following its defeat by East
2 CONC ern Jin forces at the Battle of Fei River, Tuoba Gui took the opportunity to reestablish Dai in 386. He soon changed the dynasty's name to Wei and declared himself a prince. He was initially a vassal of the Later Yan, but after de
2 CONC feating Later Yan emperor Murong Bao in 397 and seizing most of Later Yan's territory, he claimed the imperial title in 398.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Daowu was commonly regarded as a brilliant general, but cruel and arbitrary in his rule, particularly toward the end of his reign. In 409, as he considered killing his concubine Consort Helan, his son Tuoba Shao (拓拔紹) the 
2 CONC Prince of Qinghe, by Consort Helan, killed him, but was soon defeated by the crown prince Tuoba Si (Emperor Mingyuan) who then took the throne.
