1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Pei Songzhi''' 裴松之 (372–451), ''zi'' '''Shiqi''' 世期, Liu-Song period historian.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pei Songzhi came from the distinguished Pei family of Wenxi 聞喜 in Hedong 河東 (modern Wenxi, Shanxi). At the end of the Western Jin, some members of the Pei family left their ancestral home and took up residence in the south. Pei So
2 CONC ngzhi’s grandfather, Pei Mei 裴昧 (b. ca. 310), served in the Eastern Jin court as grand master for splendid happiness. His father, Pei Gui 裴珪 (b. ca. 341), was a supernumerary official at the Jin court.
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known of Pei Songzhi’s early life. He had scholarly interests as a young boy and reputedly mastered both the ''Lun yu'' and ''Mao shi'' by the age of eight. In 391, at the age of twenty, Pei Songzhi began his official ca
2 CONC reer as general of the palace at the court of Emperor Xiaowu (r. 372–396). Pei’s uncle, Yu Kai 庾楷 (d. 399), inspector of Yu province, who was stationed in Jiangling 江陵 (modern Jiangling, Hubei), tried to have Pei assigned as gover
2 CONC nor of Xinye 新野 (modern Xinye, Henan). However, because of some difficulty, Pei was unable to take up the post. If he had done so, he might have been killed when Yu Kai and Wang Gong 王恭 (d. 398) led a coup against the throne in 39
2 CONC 8–99. Yu Kai was executed by Huan Xuan 桓玄 in 399.
2 CONT
2 CONT At an unspecified later time (at least before 405), Pei was appointed supernumerary gentleman cavalier attendant, which was a post given only to members of “pure and honorable” families. Ca. 405, Pei left the court and took up the
2 CONC  position of magistrate of Guzhang 故鄣 in Wuxing 吳興 commandery (modern Wuxing, Zhejiang) where he earned merit for his administration. He soon was recalled to the court as gentleman of the masters of writing for the Ministry of Sac
2 CONC rifices. While serving in this post, he presented to the court a petition recommending that private erecting of stele inscriptions be banned.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 409, Liu Yu 劉裕 (420–479) began a series of military campaigns that eventually led to his overthrowing the Eastern Jin and establishing the Liu Song dynasty (420–479). Pei Songzhi served Liu Yu in various capacities, including m
2 CONC aster of records in Sizhou 司州 (modern Luoyang) after Liu Yu became inspector there in 416. He also administered Luoyang after Liu Yu captured it. He was soon summoned back to the capital in Jiankang, where he served in various cap
2 CONC acities, including forerunner of the heir designate, and professor at the national university.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under the second Liu Song ruler, Emperor Wen (r. 424–453), Pei Songzhi continued to serve in the imperial court. In 426, Emperor Wen sent sixteen grand commissioners, including Pei, to tour various areas of the realm. Pei was sent
2 CONC  to Xiangzhou 湘州 (modern Hunan). He was then appointed gentleman-in-attendance of the palace writers and senior impartial and just of both Sizhou and Jizhou. In 428, Emperor Wen commissioned Pei Songzhi to compile a commentary to 
2 CONC the Sanguo zhi, a work that he completed and presented to the throne on 8 September 429. Emperor Wen declared the work “imperishable.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Pei held several local positions, including that of governor of Yongjia 永嘉 (modern Wenzhou, Zhejiang). It is not clear whether this was an exile. Pei retired in 437, but he soon returned to the court where he rose to grand palace 
2 CONC grandee and professor of the national university. He also was given charge of completing the Song history begun by He Chengtian 何承天 (370–447), who had died before finishing it. Pei Songzhi died in 451 at the age of eighty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pei Songzhi’s son, Pei Yin 裴駰 (fl. 438), also was a historian. He is well known for his commentary to the ''Shi ji''.
2 CONT
2 CONT The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists Pei Songzhi’s collected works in thirteen ''juan'' and mentions a Liang catalogue that contained a twenty-one-''juan'' version. Both Tang histories list it in thirty ''juan''.
2 CONC  This was lost in the Song. Pei Songzhi also wrote a history of the Pei family, ''Pei shi jia zhuan'' 裴氏家傳, which also is lost. He also compiled two annalistic histories, ''Jin ji'' 晉紀 (Jin annals) and ''Song Yuanjia qiju zhu'' 宋元
2 CONC 嘉起居注 (Diary of activity and repose for the Song Yuanjia period) neither of which is extant.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pei Songzhi’s only fully extant work is his commentary to the ''Sanguo zhi''. In his petition that accompanied his completed manuscript of the commentary, Pei indicates that one of his main concerns was to “obtain all of the matte
2 CONC rs that Chen Shou did not include in his account but should have recorded in order to fill in the gaps in Chen Shou’s history.” Thus, Pei Songzhi’s commentary mainly consists of citations from as many as 258 sources. In some place
2 CONC s, Pei Songzhi offers his own interpretation of events or persons. He also provides 98 phonetic glosses. Contrary to common opinion, Pei’s commentary is not longer than the original text. Wang Tingqia, Cui Shuting, and Cutter-Crow
2 CONC ell have shown that Chen Shou’s text has some 360,000 characters as opposed to some 320,000 characters in Pei Songzhi’s commentary.
2 CONT
2 CONT David R. Knechtges
2 CONT
2 CONT Citation
2 CONT * 裴松之 Pei Songzhi, Brill’s Chinese Reference Shelf. Brill Online, 2017. http://chinesereferenceshelf.brillonline.com/ancient-literature/entries/SIM-300297
