0 @N6000000002188193948@ NOTE Emperor Gong of Western Wei
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT (Xi) Wei Gongdi ((?)???)
1 CONT Family name: Initially Yuan (?, yuán),
1 CONT later Tuoba (??, tuò bá)
1 CONT (changed 554)
1 CONT Given name: Kuo (?, kuò)
1 CONT Posthumous name: Gong (?, gong),
1 CONT literary meaning: "respectful"
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Gong of Western Wei ((?)???) (537-557), personal name né Yuan Kuo (??), later changed to Tuoba Kuo (???), was the last emperor of the Chinese/Xianbei state Western Wei -- a branch successor state to Northern Wei. He was ma
1 CONC de emperor in 554 after his older brother Emperor Fei was deposed by the paramount general Yuwen Tai. He carried little actual power, and in 556, after Yuwen Tai's death, Yuwen Tai's nephew Yuwen Hu, serving as guardian to Yuwen T
1 CONC ai's son Yuwen Jue, forced Emperor Gong to yield the throne to Yuwen Jue, ending Western Wei and starting Northern Zhou. The former emperor was killed in 557. Because Northern Wei's other branch successor state, Eastern Wei, had f
1 CONC allen in 551, Emperor Gong can be regarded as Northern Wei's final emperor as well.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Background
1 CONT     * 2 Reign
1 CONT     * 3 Death
1 CONT     * 4 Personal information
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Background
1 CONT
1 CONT Yuan Kuo was born in 537, as the fourth son of Emperor Wen. His mother's name is not recorded in history. In 548, Emperor Wen created him the Prince of Qi. However, nothing else is known about his activities during his father's re
1 CONC ign, during which the paramount general Yuwen Tai largely controlled power. Yuwen's hold on power appeared to be even firmer after Emperor Wen died in 551 and was succeeded by Yuan Kuo's older brother Yuan Qin (as Emperor Fei). Du
1 CONC ring Yuan Kuo's stint as the Prince of Qi, he married the daughter of the general Ruogan Hui (???) as his wife and princess.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 554, Emperor Fei, angry over Yuwen Tai's execution of the official Yuan Lie (??) in 553, plotted to have Yuwen killed. However, the news was leaked, and Yuwen deposed him. He selected Yuan Kuo to succeed Emperor Fei (as Emperor
1 CONC  Gong). Contemporaneously, Yuwen changed the imperial clan's surname from Yuan back to Tuoba, reversing the change that Emperor Xiaowen ordered in 496.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reign
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Gong appeared to be even more powerless than his father and brother, and the power remained in Yuwen's hands. He created his wife, Princess Ruogan, empress. Later in 554, Yuwen poisoned the deposed Emperor Fei to death. La
1 CONC ter that year, an army dispatched by Yuwen and commanded by Yu Jin (??) captured rival Liang Dynasty's capital Jiangling (??, in modern Jingzhou, Hubei), capturing and executing Liang's Emperor Yuan. In spring 555, Emperor Gong cr
1 CONC eated Xiao Cha, a nephew of Emperor Yuan, the Emperor of Liang (as Emperor Xuan), but Xiao Cha was not recognized by most of Liang generals and officials, who instead recognized Xiao Yuanming, a candidate supported by Northern Qi,
1 CONC  as emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later in 555, Yuwen required Tuoba Yu (???) the Prince of Huai'an to submit to Emperor Gong a propose to have the imperial princes reduced in rank to dukes, a proposal that Emperor Gong approved. In spring 556, pursuant to Yuwen's
1 CONC  desire to reorganize the governmental structure in accordance with the Zhou Dynasty governmental structure, the imperial government was reorganized into six departments.
1 CONT
1 CONT in fall 556, while Yuwen Tai was on a tour of the norther provinces, he became ill at Qiantun Mountain (???, in modern Guyuan, Ningxia). He summoned his nephew Yuwen Hu to Qiantun and entrusted the affairs of the state as well as 
1 CONC his sons to Yuwen Hu. He soon died, and Yuwen Tai's 14-year-old son Yuwen Jue took over his titles, while Yuwen Hu took the reigns of the state. Emperor Gong was subsequently forced to create Yuwen Jue the greater title of Duke of
1 CONC  Zhou.
1 CONT
1 CONT In spring 557, Yuwen Hu, believing that Yuwen Jue's youth meant that he needed to take imperial title to affirm his authority, forced Emperor Gong to yield the throne to Yuwen Jue, ending Western Wei and starting Northern Zhou.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death
1 CONT
1 CONT Yuwen Jue (Emperor Xiaomin) initially created Tuoba Kuo the Duke of Song. However, less than two months after Tuoba Kuo yielded the throne, he was killed. His wife, the former Empress Ruogan, became a Buddhist nun.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Personal information
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Father
1 CONT           o Emperor Wen of Western Wei
1 CONT     * Wife
1 CONT           o Empress Ruogan (created 554)
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Emperor Fei of Western Wei Emperor of Northern Wei (Western)
1 CONT 554-556 Succeeded by
1 CONT None (dynasty destroyed)
1 CONT Emperor of China (Western)
1 CONT 554-556 Succeeded by
1 CONT Emperor Xiaomin of Northern Zhou
0 @N6000000002188195798@ NOTE Empress Murong (Daowu)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Murong (????, personal name unknown), formally Empress Daowu (????), was an empress of the Chinese/Xianbei dynasty Northern Wei Dynasty. Her husband was the founding emperor Emperor Daowu (Tuoba Gui).
1 CONT
1 CONT She was the youngest daughter of the Later Yan emperor Murong Bao (Emperor Huimin). When Northern Wei launched a major attack against Later Yan in 396, causing Murong Bao to abandon the capital Zhongshan (??, in modern Baoding, He
1 CONC bei) in 397, she was unable to follow her father in flight, and when Zhongshan fell to Northern Wei later that year, she was captured by Tuoba Gui and made one of his consorts. In 400, after he had declared imperial title, he cons
1 CONC idered whom to create empress. He had favored Consort Liu, the daughter of the Xiongnu chief Liu Toujuan (???) the most, and she also bore his oldest son Tuoba Si. However, the Tuoba Tribe's traditions dictated that when the ruler
1 CONC  were to consider whom to make his wife, he was to make the candidates try to forge golden statues by hand, as a way of discerning divine favor. Consort Liu was unable to forge a golden statue, while Consort Murong was able to, an
1 CONC d so was created empress.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nothing more is known about Empress Murong. She was implied to be still alive in 402, when Later Qin's emperor Yao Xing rejected Emperor Daowu's overture to marry one of his daughters, because Yao Xing's refusal was based on his i
1 CONC nformation that Emperor Daowu already had Empress Murong as empress.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT None (dynasty founded) Empress of Northern Wei
1 CONT 400-409? Succeeded by
1 CONT Empress Helian
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Empress Yang Empress of China (Inner Mongolia)
1 CONT 400-409?
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Empress Duan of Later Yan Empress of China (Shanxi/Hebei)
1 CONT 400-409?
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Empress Duan Jifei of Southern Yan Empress of China (Huatai region)
1 CONT 400-409?
0 @N6000000002188195764@ NOTE Princess Dowager Helan
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Dowager Helan (????, personal name unknown) (351-396), formally Empress Xianming (????, literally "the wise and understanding empress), was, according to official history of the Chinese/Xianbei dynasty Northern Wei, the m
1 CONC other of the founding emperor Emperor Daowu (Tuoba Gui). Her husband was Tuoba Gui's father Tuoba Shi (???), the heir apparent of the Dai prince Tuoba Shiyijian (?????). (However, for reasons why this account may have its problems
1 CONC , see here -- in particular, note below with regard to description of the parentage of Tuoba Gu (???).)
1 CONT
1 CONT Lady Helan was a daughter of the Helan tribal chief Helan Yegan (????), an important general under Tuoba Shiyijian. Because of her beauty, Tuoba Shiyijian selected her to be the wife of his son Tuoba Shi. In 371, when the general 
1 CONC Baba Jin (???) attempted to assassinate Tuoba Shiyijian, Tuoba Shi took the blunt of the assault personally to protect his father, and he died later that year. After Tuoba Shi's death, Heiress Apparent Helan gave birth to Tuoba Gu
1 CONC i. In 377, after Tuoba Shiyijian was assassinated by his son Tuoba Shijun (????), Dai fell to Former Qin forces. Initially, she took Tuoba Gui to Helan tribe to be taken under the protection of her brother Helan Na (???), but late
1 CONC r went to be under the protection of the Xiongnu chief Liu Kuren (???).
1 CONT
1 CONT Not much was known about Lady Helan or her son for a number of years. In 384, after Former Qin had began to collapse in light of its defeat at the Battle of Fei River, Liu Kuren tried to aid the Former Qin prince Fu Pi, but was as
1 CONC sassinated by his own general Muyu Chang (???). His son Liu Xian (??) later tried to kill Tuoba Gui, but Lady Helan heard the news and allowed her son to flee. Later, both were again at Helan tribe, when similarly her brother Hela
1 CONC n Rangan (????) tried to kill Tuoba Gui, but stopped after she interceded.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Tuoba Gui redeclared the Dai state as its prince with Helan Na's support in 386 and later changed the state's name to Wei, Lady Helan was honored as princess dowager. In 391, when Tuoba Gui sent his younger brother and her s
1 CONC on Tuoba Gu as a messenger to Later Yan to pay tribute, Tuoba Gu was detained by Later Yan, and it was said that she grew ill from her worries for him. She died in 396. (The problem with this account is that if Tuoba Shi had died 
1 CONC before Tuoba Gui's death, than she could not have had an even younger son by him -- but of course, she might have married another member of the Tuoba clan, perhaps Tuoba Shi's younger brother Tuoba Han (???), for Tuoba Gu was also
1 CONC  referred to as the younger brother of Tuoba Han's son Tuoba Yi (???).) After Tuoba Gui declared himself emperor around the new year 399, he posthumously honored Tuoba Shi as an emperor and her as an empress.
0 @N6000000002188195907@ NOTE Tuoba Shiyijian
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Tuoba Shiyijian (Chinese: ?????; pinyin: Tuòbá Shíyìjiàn) (320-376) was the last prince of the Tuoba Dai and ruled from 338 to 376 when Dai was conquered by the Former Qin. He was the son of Tuoba Yulü (????) and the younger broth
1 CONC er of Tuoba Yihuai (????), whom he succeeded in 338. In 340 he moved the capital to Shengle (??) (near modern Holingol county (????) of Hohhot, Inner Mongolia). His grandson Tuoba Gui would go on to found the Northern Wei Dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Era name
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Jianguo (?? jiàn guó) 338-376
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Personal information
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Father
1 CONT           o Tuoba Yulü (????)
1 CONT     * Wife
1 CONT           o Princess Murong, a princess of Former Yan
1 CONT     * Children
1 CONT           o Tuoba Shi (???), the Heir Apparent (d. 371)[1]
1 CONT           o Tuoba Shijun (????)
1 CONT           o Tuoba Han (???)
1 CONT           o Tuoba Shoujiu (????)
1 CONT           o Tuoba Gegen (????)
1 CONT           o Tuoba Digan (????)
1 CONT           o Tuoba Quduo (????)
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Tuoba Yihuai Prince of Dai
1 CONT 338-376 Succeeded by
1 CONT None (dynasty destroyed)
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ However, see Emperor Daowu of Northern Wei for problems with placing Tuoba Shi as a son of Tuoba Shiyijian. It may very well be that Tuoba Gui (Emperor Daowu) was a son of Tuoba Shiyijian, not a grandson (through Tuoba Shi
1 CONC ).
0 @N6000000001353874392@ NOTE Khosrow I, byname KHOSROW ANUSHIRVAN (Persian: "Khosrow of the Immortal Soul"), or KHOSROW THE JUST (d. AD 579), Persian king who ruled theSasanian empire from 531 to 579 and was remembered as a great reformer and patron of the ar
1 CONC ts and scholarship.
1 CONT Rise to power.
1 CONT Little is known of the early life of Khosrow beyond legends. One story says that when Khosrow's father, King Kavadh, took refuge with the Hephthalites, eastern neighbours of Iran, on the way (near the town ofNishapur) he married a
1 CONC  peasant's daughter, who gave birth to Khosrow.At his father's death Khosrow did not at first succeed him, but in a struggle for the throne he was successful and put to death his brothers. At the end of his father's reign, great s
1 CONC ocial disorders had occurred because of a religious revolution of a sect called the Mazdakites. Khosrow first restored order and then launched reforms to transform the declining Sasanian empire.
1 CONT Reforms.
1 CONT The reform of taxation was the most important of his actions, and it was probably copied from the Roman system inaugurated by the emperor Diocletian. Previously in the Sasanian empire taxes had been levied on the yield of land; Kh
1 CONC osrow established a fixed sum rather than a yearly variation. Other taxes were introduced that brought stability to the income of the state and were also fairer to those who paid the taxes. Khosrow's program of taxes lasted into I
1 CONC slamic times.
1 CONT Khosrow also reorganized the Sasanian bureaucracy, and the system of ministries, or divans, under a prime minister is said to have been initiated by him. He was fortunate during most of his reign in having a capable prime minister
1 CONC  called Bozorgmehr, who became famous in story and legend for his wisdom and abilities.
1 CONT Under Khosrow the process of decentralization of the power of the monarch was reversed, and the lower aristocracy, or knights, called dihqans, grew in importance at the expense of the great feudal lords, who had been more powerful
1 CONC  under Khosrow's predecessors. It is difficult toknow how many changes really can be attributed to Khosrow's reign andhow many are arbitrarily assigned to him because of his place in history. Whether the religiously sanctioned div
1 CONC ision of society into priests, warriors, bureaucracy, and common folk was codified under his reign, as claimed by some sources, is difficult to determine.
1 CONT Military campaigns.
1 CONT Khosrow also reorganized the army and appointed four chief commandersto guard the four frontiers of Iran. On the frontier against the Byzantines and their Arab allies in the Syrian Desert, against the peoplesof the steppes of sout
1 CONC hern Russia at the town of Derbent between the Caucasus Mountains and the Caspian Sea, and to the east of the sea in the present Turkmen steppes, Khosrow built defensive walls. The army, however, did not remain on the defensive, f
1 CONC or Khosrow's reign is notedfor his wars against the Byzantines. In 540 Antioch was conquered andheld for a short time by Khosrow. He brought many prisoners from Antioch and settled them in a new town near his capital of Ctesiphon,
1 CONC  modeled on old Antioch. In the east, Khosrow, in alliance with the Turks,a new power in Central Asia, crushed the Hephthalites, and he established a hegemony over many of their principalities. The Sasanian frontier in the east re
1 CONC ached the Amu Darya (Oxus River) during his reign.
1 CONT He also fought extensively in Armenia and Lazica in the Caucasus. Furthermore, under his reign a Sasanian army conquered Yemen. Khosrow relied on a professional army more than his predecessors, who had summoned feudal levies when 
1 CONC they set out to war.
1 CONT Patron of culture.
1 CONT Khosrow was also a great patron of culture, and in 529, when the ancient academy of Athens was closed, a number of Greek philosophers migrated to the Sasanian empire, where they were well received by the ruler. The later famous me
1 CONC dical school of Gondeshapur was probably startedin Khosrow's reign, and the famous physician Burzoe is supposed to have been sent to India by Khosrow to gather Sanskrit books of learning to be translated into the Middle Persian la
1 CONC nguage. The game of chess reportedly was also brought by him from India. Astronomy and astrology flourished at the court of Khosrow, and one star table (called the zij-i Shahriyar), which was the basis of many later Islamic tables
1 CONC , is said to have originated during the reign of Khosrow. Several works of Middle Persian, such as the Book of Deeds of Ardashir (Karnamak), are attributed to this period. Likewise, some scholars claim that the codification of the
1 CONC  Avesta, the sacred book of the Zoroastrian religion, as well as the creation of a special Avestan alphabet to record the text,took place at the order of Khosrow. Further, it is supposed that the stories and legends of ancient Ira
1 CONC n were gathered into a Khwatay-namak("Book of Kings") in the time of Khosrow and thus provided the sourcefor Ferdowsi's immortal epic much later. Some of the names found in Ferdowsi's Shah-nameh appear among the royal family of Kh
1 CONC osrow, which indicates at the least an interest on the part of the monarch in ancient legends.
1 CONT Perhaps more than fact, the stories told about Khosrow have made his name famous in history. Almost any pre-Islamic structure in Iran whoseorigin is unknown will be attributed to Khosrow by the simple folk. Undoubtedly he built ma
1 CONC ny bridges, roads, and palaces, but much more isassigned to him in legend. The famous palace with the huge arch, called Taq Kisra, in Ctesiphon, near modern Baghdad, is said to date from Khosrow I, but this is uncertain. Several c
1 CONC ollections of wise sayings of this monarch, as well as stories about him, have been preserved in Arabic or New Persian versions. In them his reputation for justice as well as wisdom is constantly cited. The splendour of the court 
1 CONC and theglory of his reign provided models for the later 'Abbasid court in Baghdad, and many of the institutions established by Khosrow were maintained in Islamic times, when Khosrow was hailed as the model pre-Islamic ruler to be 
1 CONC emulated by Muslim princes. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97, CHOSROES I]
1 CONT Chosroes I., "the blessed" (Anushirvan), A.D. 531-579, successor of Kavadh I., and the most famous of the Sassanid kings. At the beginning of his reign he concluded an "eternal" peace with the emperor Justinian, who wanted to be f
1 CONC ree for the conquest of Africa and Sicily. But successes against the Vandals and Goths caused Chosroes to renew the warin 540. He invaded Syria and took Antioch, and during the next years fought successfully in Lazica or Lazistan 
1 CONC (the ancient Colchis), on the Black sea, and in Mesopotamia. In 545 an armistice was concluded and in 562 a peace for 50 years in which the Persians left Lazistan to the Romans, and the Romans paid subsidies to Persia. Meanwhile i
1 CONC n the east the Hephthalites had been attacked by the Turks, who now appear for the first time in history. Chosroes united with them and conquered Bactria, while he left the country north of the Oxus to the Turks. He also assisted 
1 CONC the dynasts of Yemen to expel the Ethiopians and Yemen became dependent on Persia. In 571 a new war with Rome broke out about Armenia, in which Chosroes conquered the fortress Dara on the Euphrates, invaded Syria and Cappadocia, a
1 CONC nd returned with large booty. Duringthe negotiations with the emperor Tiberius, Chosroes died in 579, andwas succeeded by his son Hormizd IV.
1 CONT Although Chosroes had extirpated the heretical Persian sect of the Mazdakites he was a tolerant adherent of Zoroastrian orthodoxy. He introduced a system of taxation based upon a survey of landed possessions begun by his father. [
1 CONC Encyclopaedia Britannica, 1961ed., Vol. 5, p. 524B, KHOSROW I]
