0 @N6000000002188198036@ NOTE Princess Duan (Wenming)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Duan (???, personal name unknown) was the wife of the Chinese/Xianbei state Former Yan founder Murong Huang. She was likely a daughter of a Duan tribal chief, as the Duans and the Murongs intermarried frequently. When Mur
1 CONC ong Huang claimed the title "Prince of Yan" in 337, she was created princess. There was no further mentioning of her during the reign of Murong Huang or his son and successor Murong Jun, who was likely her son, but there is no con
1 CONC clusive evidence. It is not known when she died. Sometime after Murong Jun declared himself emperor in 352, she was honored as an empress, although it was not clear whether she was still alive at that point. After Former Yan's fal
1 CONC l in 370 and subsequent "restoration" as Later Yan in 384 by Murong Chui, the son of her husband Murong Huang but by his concubine Consort Lan, not her, Murong Chui excluded her from her husband's temple and enshrined his mother C
1 CONC onsort Lan instead.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT None (dynasty founded) Princess of Former Yan
1 CONT 337-348? Succeeded by
1 CONT Empress Kezuhun
0 @N6000000002188198025@ NOTE Murong Hui
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Murong Hui (???), (269—333) Xianbei chief and Duke Xiang of Liaodong, posthumously honored as Prince Wuxuan of Yan.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Murong Hui had initially been a Xianbei chief who fought Jin forces during the late reign of Emperor Wu of Jin, Jin's founding emperor, but he submitted as a Jin vassal in 289. Under constant attack by fellow Xianbei chief Duan Ji
1 CONC e (??) of the Duan tribe, he humbly sought peace with the Duan and married one of Duan Jie's daughters. From this union came Murong Huang (in 297) and two of his younger brothers, Murong Ren (???) and Murong Zhao (???).
1 CONT
1 CONT During Murong Hui's rule as tribal chief, the Jin Dynasty's central government was in constant turmoil and eventually collapsed due to infighting and agrarian rebellions, the strongest of which was the Xiongnu state Han Zhao. As a
1 CONC  result, many refugees arrived in the relatively safe domain of Murong Hui's, and as he treated the ethnically Han refugees with kindness, most chose to stay, greatly strengthening his power, and as Jin forces in the north gradual
1 CONC ly fell to Han Zhao's capable general Shi Le (who later established his own independent state Later Zhao), Murong Hui became the only domain in northern China still under titular Jin rule, carrying the Jin-bestowed title of the Du
1 CONC ke of Liaodong. He entrusted Murong Huang with many important military tasks such as fighting the powerful fellow Xianbei Yuwen tribe in 320 and 325. In early 322, Murong Hui named Murong Huang heir apparent. However, Murong Hui a
1 CONC lso greatly favored Murong Huang's brothers Murong Ren, Murong Zhao, and particularly Murong Han (???), who was regarded very highly as a general. Murong Huang became jealous and suspicious of these brothers, feelings which became
1 CONC  known to them and which would sow the seeds of future troubles.
0 @N6000000002188099869@ NOTE Emperor Ruizong of Tang
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Ruizong of Tang 
1 CONT Birth and death: June 22, 662–July 13, 716 
1 CONT Family name: Li (?), later Wu¹ (?),
1 CONT then later back to Li² (?) 
1 CONT Given name: Xulun³ (??), later Lun4 (?),
1 CONT then later Dan5 (?), then
1 CONT back to Lun6 (?), and
1 CONT eventually back to Dan7 (?) 
1 CONT Dates of reign (1st time): Feb. 27, 684–Oct. 19, 6908 
1 CONT Dates of reign (2nd time): July 25, 710–Sept. 8, 7129 
1 CONT Dynasty: Tang (?) 
1 CONT Temple name: Ruizong (??) 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT
1 CONT (short) Never used short 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT
1 CONT (full) Emperor Xuanzhen Dasheng
1 CONT Daxing Xiao10
1 CONT ????????? 
1 CONT General note: Dates given here are in the Julian calendar.
1 CONT They are not in the proleptic Gregorian calendar. 
1 CONT ——— 
1 CONT 1. In October 690, when he ceded the imperial throne to his mother
1 CONT Empress Wu and was made heir to the throne, his family name was
1 CONT changed into Wu which was the family name of his mother. 
1 CONT 2. His original family name Li, the family name of the emperors of
1 CONT the Tang Dynasty, was restored at a date not indicated by ancient
1 CONT Chinese historical books, perhaps in 705 at the fall of Empress Wu. 
1 CONT 3. This is the original given name of Emperor Ruizong. The literal
1 CONT meaning is "disk of the sun appearing above the horizon
1 CONT at daybreak". 
1 CONT 4. Had his name shortened into Lun ("disk") in December 669. 
1 CONT 5. Had his name changed into Dan (literal meaning: "dawn")
1 CONT in August 683. 
1 CONT 6. Had his name changed back to Lun when he ceded the imperial
1 CONT throne to his mother Empress Wu in October 690 and was made
1 CONT heir to the throne. 
1 CONT 7. Had his name changed back to Dan after he was reduced to a
1 CONT princely rank in 698 when his older brother Zhongzong replaced
1 CONT him as heir to the throne. This name became his taboo name when
1 CONT he was restored as emperor in 710. 
1 CONT 8. Ceded the imperial throne to his mother Empress Wu who
1 CONT became "emperor" while Ruizong was made heir to the throne. 
1 CONT 9. Abdicated in favor of his son Emperor Xuanzong and received
1 CONT the title Taishang Huang (???). In practice, continued to rule
1 CONT through his son until the palace coup of July 29, 713, and officially
1 CONT ceded full power to his son on July 30. 
1 CONT 10. Final version of his posthumous name as given in 754. 
1 CONT Emperor Ruizong (June 22, 662 – July 13, 716), personal name Li Dan, was the fifth and ninth emperor of Tang Dynasty. He was the eighth son of Emperor Gaozong and Empress Wu.
1 CONT
1 CONT In February 684, his mother Empress Wu demoted his older brother Emperor Zhongzong who had attempted to rule free of his mother, and named him emperor. Ruizong, however, was under control of his mother and didn't have any real pow
1 CONC er. In October 690 he ceded the imperial throne to his mother who was installed as "emperor" (huangdi, ??), the only woman in Chinese history ever to rule as emperor. Ruizong was reduced to the position of "heir to the throne". In
1 CONC  the following years, Empress Wu's family members (from the Wu family) tried to have one of them named heir to the throne, but the empress resisted these calls. Eventually, in October 698, faced with foreign invasion and dissatisf
1 CONC action at home, Empress Wu yielded to officials still loyal to the Li family (the Tang imperial family overthrown by the empress) and so she clearly hinted that she would be succeeded by a member of the Li family by appointing her
1 CONC  son the demoted Emperor Zhongzong as heir to the throne. On that occasion, Ruizong was reduced to a princely rank.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ruizong lived the next twelve years away from political intrigues and did not take part in the coup that saw the overthrow of Empress Wu and the restoration of Zhongzong as emperor in 705. The five years of Zhongzong's reign were 
1 CONC dominated by Zhongzong's empress consort, Empress Wei (???), and her lover Wu Sansi (???), the nephew of the deceased Empress Wu, who both dominated the weak emperor. In the beginning of July 710, Emperor Zhongzong died, allegedly
1 CONC  poisoned by Empress Wei who then named Zhongzong's only surviving son, a teenager called Li Chongmao, as emperor. A mere two weeks later, Princess Taiping, the older sister of Ruizong, launched a coup with the help of Ruizong's t
1 CONC hird son, Li Longji (the future Emperor Xuanzong), which resulted in the death of Empress Wei and the overthrow of the teenage emperor. Ruizong was unwillingly restored as emperor on July 25 by his sister and his son. Li Longji, w
1 CONC ho had proved his worth during the coup, was named crown prince with the consent of Ruizong's eldest son.
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon, however, tensions between Princess Taiping and Li Longji mounted. Princess Taiping, who had many supporters at court, hoped to ascend the imperial throne as her mother Empress Wu had done. Ruizong exiled his sister to the pr
1 CONC ovinces, hoping to defuse tension, but Li Longji, who feared that Princess Taiping would hold him responsible for her exile and would have him murdered, asked his father to recall the princess, who was allowed to return to the imp
1 CONC erial court. Eventually, in September 712, a disgruntled Ruizong, tired of court feuds, abdicated in favor of his son the crown prince Li Longji. Suddenly it was Princess Taiping who was at risk of falling victim to her nephew the
1 CONC  new emperor. The princess, alarmed by Ruizong's decision, managed to have the court name him Taishang Huang (???, sometimes translated as "retired emperor", or "emperor emeritus"), a position in which Ruizong was to keep effectiv
1 CONC e control of power with the new emperor Xuangzong only formally attending court audiences and ceremonies.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fearing for her future, Princess Taiping launched a coup in the end of July 713 to overthrow Emperor Xuanzong. As she was a woman and could not personally lead troops, the coup failed and all her supporters were killed. Princess T
1 CONC aiping fled to a Buddhist monastery in the mountains. She returned three days later and was allowed to commit suicide at home. On July 30, one day after the coup, Ruizong officially announced he was ceding full power to his son Em
1 CONC peror Xuanzong. Ruizong was allowed to keep the title Taishang Huang but lived away from politics until his death three years later in 716.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Emperor Zhongzong Emperor of Tang China
1 CONT 684–690 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Empress Wu 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Emperor Shang Emperor of Tang China
1 CONT 710–712 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Emperor Xuanzong 
0 @N6000000002188193597@ NOTE Shirin
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Shirin (? – 628 a.d.) (Persian: ?????) was an Armenian princess and wife of the Sassanid Persian Shahanshah (king of kings), Khosrau II. In the revolution after the death of Khosrau's father Hormizd IV, the General Bahram Chobin t
1 CONC ook power over the Persian empire. Shirin fled with Khosrau to Syria where they lived under the protection of Byzantine emperor Maurice. In 591, Khosrau returned to Persia to take control of the empire and Shirin was made queen. S
1 CONC he used her new influence to support the Christian minority in Iran, but the political situation demanded that she do so discreetly. Initially she belonged to the Church of the East, the so-named Nestorians, but later she joined t
1 CONC he monophysitic western-Syrian church. After conquering Jerusalem in 614, the Persians supposedly captured the cross of Jesus and brought it to their capital Ctesiphon, where Shirin took the cross in her palace.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] In Persian Literature
1 CONT After the fall of Khosrau, Firdausi remembered Shirin in his epic, the Shahnama. Around 1180 the Persian poet Nezami wrote of her alleged love for the master builder Farhad in his epic Chosroes and Shirin. This story grew to be a 
1 CONC myth with Shirin and Farhad being symbols of pure, unrequited love.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the myth Shirin and Khosrau fell in love by seeing each other in portraits, but their first meeting was long delayed. Before Shirin agreed to marry Khusraw, there were obstacles to be overcome, including the vanquishi
1 CONC ng of Farhad, a rival for Shirin’s affections.
1 CONT
1 CONT The long standing myth spread to other ethnic literature, living on even as far as Europe with Goethe’s West-oestlicher Divan. Shirin is also mentioned in Scheherazade's The Book of One Thousand and One Nights on the 390th night w
1 CONC ith the story of Kosrau and Shirin with a fisherman.
1 CONT
1 CONT “ The romance of Khusraw and Shirin is a classic text of Persian literature. It tells the story of the courtship of the pre-Islamic king Khusraw Parviz and the Armenian princess Shirin. The poet Nizami created the romance for a lo
1 CONC cal ruler in north-west Iran in 1184 and from the 15th century manuscripts containing the poem were often illustrated with miniature paintings. [1] ” 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT Khosrau Parviz 
1 CONT Sassanid Empire 
1 CONT Chosroes and Shirin 
1 CONT Nezami 
1 CONT Sarkash 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Wilhelm Baum: Shirin: Christian - Queen - Myth of Love, 2004 (orig. German: Schirin. Christin - Königin - Liebesmythos. Eine spätantike Frauengestalt - Historische Realität und literarische Wirkung, 2003). 
1 CONT ^ The Romance of Khusraw and Shirin Illustrated by Riza’ ‘Abbasi (c. 1565 - 1635) 
1 CONT Gianroberto Scarcia: Scirin. La Regina dei Magi, Ed. Jaca Book, Milano, 2004. 
0 @N6000000001354427278@ NOTE Yazdegerd III (d. 651, Merv, Sasanian Empire), the last king of the Sasanian dynasty (reigned 632-651), the son of Shahryar and a grandson of Khosrow II.
1 CONT A mere child when he was placed on the throne, Yazdegerd never actually exercised power. In his first year the Arab invasion began, and in 636/637 the Battle of al-Qadisiyah on one of the Euphrates canals decided the fate of the e
1 CONC mpire. His capital, Ctesiphon, was occupied by the Arabs, and Yazdegerd fled into Media, where his generals unsuccessfully attempted to organize resistance. After the Battle of Nahavand (642), in which Sasanian forces were badly d
1 CONC efeated, Yazdegerd sought refuge in one district after another, until at last he was slain at Merv. The Parsis--Zoroastrians who immigrated to western India on the advent of Islam--still use the old Persian calendar and continue t
1 CONC o countthe years from Yazdegerd's accession. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97, YAZDEGERD]
0 @N6000000002187918539@ NOTE Yazdgerd III
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Yazdgerd III (Persian: ?????? ???, "made by God"), last king of Sassanid dynasty, a grandson of Khosrau II (590–628), who had been murdered by his son Kavadh II of Persia in 628, and was raised to the throne in 632 after a series 
1 CONC of internal conflicts.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yazdgerd III reigned as a youth and never truly exercised authority. In his first year the Arab invasion of Persia began, and in 636 the Battle of al-Qadisiyyah decided the fate of the Persian empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT Arabs occupied Ctesiphon, and the young King fled into Media. Yazdgerd III fled from one district to another, until at last he was murdered at Merv in 651. The Parsees, who use the old Persian calendar, continue to count the years
1 CONC  from his accession (era of Yazdgerd, beginning June 16, 632).
1 CONT
1 CONT Yazdgerd III's daughter Shahr Banu married Husayn ibn Ali, Muhammad's grandson, and gave birth to the fourth Shia Imam, Ali Zayn al Abidin. His son Pirooz fled to China.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT
